# go-turbo

Use `skills/go-turbo/SKILL.md` for the full workflow and focused references.

Write clear, idiomatic Go first. Preserve behavior, errors, ownership, race
freedom, cancellation, validation, deadlines, and resource cleanup. Treat
latency, throughput, and memory as workload-specific measurements.

Work in this order and stop when complexity no longer pays:

1. Remove, defer, cache, coalesce, or short-circuit work.
2. Fix the algorithm, data structure, index, and ownership model.
3. Reduce material hot-path allocation and unintended retention.
4. Remove incidental escapes confirmed by compiler diagnostics.
5. Amortize syscalls, queries, RPCs, encodes, locks, and handoffs.
6. Bound concurrency and address measured contention or backpressure.
7. Only then tune layout, pooling, the runtime, protocols, or hardware.

Apply ordinary improvements only when their preconditions are clear: capacity
from an exact or defensible bound; append-style byte APIs or
`strings.Builder`; buffered small I/O with explicit flush/error behavior;
eliminated byte/string round trips; reused immutable compiled values, clients,
and transports; copies that prevent a small view retaining a large buffer;
bounded queues, cancellation, and network deadlines.

Do not assume pointers beat values, reorder wire-visible structs casually,
preallocate from untrusted maxima, or add concurrency without a workload need.

Require a representative profile, trace, benchmark, or production metric
before `sync.Pool`, zero-copy aliasing, manual padding, lock-free code, mmap,
raw socket controls, GC knobs, PGO, `unsafe`, assembly, or SIMD. Record the
measured benefit, ownership/portability contract, and removal trigger beside a
justified complex optimization.

For comparisons, use focused benchmarks with `-benchmem -count=10` and
`benchstat`; report `ns/op`, `B/op`, `allocs/op`, Go version, architecture,
and workload. Run `go test -race` after concurrency changes. Never claim
“faster” from code inspection; say what remains unmeasured.
